3 < 1 ## The worktree-pure contract
4 < 1 
5 < 1 Every `implementation` task is a pure diff against the integration branch:
6 < 1 
7 < 1 1. **Self-contained bodies.** A task agent sees only its own body — every coordination
8 < 1    note (port assignments, shared literals) lives in the body of each task it affects,
9 < 1    never only in a preamble.
10 < 1 2. **No branch instructions.** The executor owns branching.
11 < 1 3. **Concurrency-safe proofs.** Builders run their probes at once on one machine: unique port
12 < 1    and temp path per test, no shared on-disk fixtures.
13 < 1 4. **Name only what exists.** Every path a slot cites must exist at BASE or be created by
14 < 1    a task this one derivably follows. `docs/superpowers/` is untracked (#544) and absent
15 < 1    from every sandbox, so a spec path is a reference for the reader, never something a
16 < 1    worker is asked to open — put what the worker needs from a spec into Context.
17 < 1 5. **Claims about the live world carry their evidence.** A task asserting what a live
18 < 1    system does is unverifiable from a sandbox — paste the commands and their output into
19 < 1    Context so the builder and Jev check correspondence to a record, not truth they cannot
20 < 1    reach.
21 < 1 6. **Isolate `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`** in any task that spawns the agent CLI, or it writes
22 < 1    false memories into the host project.
23 < 1 7. **Greenfield targets take the Bun + TypeScript + TinyBase defaults** — `bun install` to
24 < 1    bootstrap, `bunx tsc --noEmit && bun test` as the suite, one TinyBase store as the
25 < 1    app's state; the synced shape (store → WsSynchronizer → Durable Object) is a *TinyApp*.
27 > 1    The header lines verbatim, the `@types/bun` tsconfig gotcha, the TinyApp shape, and where
28 < 1    the restriction stops: `references/greenfield-stack.md`. A TinyApp task is proven like any
26 < 2    Both knobs verbatim, the `@types/bun` tsconfig gotcha, the TinyApp shape, and where the
27 < 2    restriction stops: `references/greenfield-stack.md`. A TinyApp task is proven like any
28 < 1    other, by `Run:` probes and the stack's `Check:` line.
29 < 2    the stack's `Check:` line, until state exams return as probes (owed on map #1248).
30 < 1 
31 < 1 ## Decomposition judgment
32 < 1 
33 < 1 Independence is a property of contracts, not of files.
34 < 1 
35 < 1 1. **Split by default.** Every piece of work that can carry its own contract — a module
36 < 1    with its own exports and its own tests — is its own task. Where a consumer would wait
37 < 1    on a producer, put the shared shape (a schema, a signature, a file format) as one
38 < 1    literal in the Context of every task that touches it, and give each side a probe that
39 < 1    pins the literal, so an implementation that drifts from it goes red. A `Consumes:` of a sibling's `Produces:` orders the two,
40 < 1    and a `Create:` a sibling task later `Modify:`s is the same kind of fact; a shared
41 < 1    literal orders neither, so prefer the literal wherever the consumer only needs the
42 < 1    shape. Workers have no shared memory — a chain of two tasks is two strangers in
43 < 1    sequence, not one mind holding a design — so a chain buys no coherence, only the wait.
44 < 1    One thing a literal cannot stand in for is the file itself: a probe that imports a sibling's created module
45 < 1    (`from tests.trends_fixtures import …`, `import('./lib/a.mjs')`) is a `proof-run` edge
46 < 1    the parser derives and the engine keeps hard under live pairs (#1265), so write the
47 < 1    probe as it is and list nothing under `Modify:` to force the wait — the wait is derived.
48 < 1 2. **Write no ordering.** An author writes no ordering: the parser derives the edges, the
49 < 1    Flock seeds its board with them, and a task becomes claimable once every task it depends
50 < 1    on is done; every other task is claimable at once, and builders merge each other's
51 < 1    published work continuously, so a same-file pair meets in the weave, not in a queue.
52 < 1    `Consumes:`/`Produces:` bullets are still written exactly, one symbol per bullet,
53 < 1    because they are how a pair is found — but the chain they imply is derived, never
54 < 1    authored, and there is no width to state and no rationale line to write. An edge an
55 < 1    author takes only to keep two same-file edits apart — not because a sibling needs the
56 < 1    other's runtime behaviour — is a defect: on run-193 the author chained the engine task
57 < 1    behind the hunk-picker task to keep two import inserts from meeting at merge, and the
58 < 1    consumer waited on a producer it needed nothing from — about nine minutes of clock lost
59 < 1    (n=1 run, 2026-09-18).
60 < 1 3. **Let same-file edits stand.** Builders merge each other's published work continuously
61 < 1    through the weave, so concurrent same-file *text* writes meet there, and a shared hot
62 < 1    file is never a reason to reshape a plan — let colliding `Modify` lines collide.
63 < 1    Non-text (binary, symlink) same-file pairs are ordered automatically. Blast radius
64 < 1    follows the contract, not the file: a task that changes a `Produces:` shape owns every
65 < 1    strict-equality pin of it, in any sibling's file — list that file in its own Files
66 < 1    block. One shape does not merge cleanly, though: N tasks that each add one line to one
67 < 1    list are N **adjacent inserts at one location**, which merge as a conflict a builder
68 < 1    must stop and resolve — run-12 (2026-09-05, PR #662) had five tasks each append one
69 < 1    registration line to one registry file and spent 3.4 worker-minutes ordering five lines
70 < 1    any order would have satisfied. Give each such task its **own region or file**: a
71 < 1    registration is a new file discovered by glob, never an appended line.
72 < 1    And when two tasks list one file, each of them carries a
73 < 1    Run: probe of what that file must keep beyond its own change: run-277's two tasks each removed one name from one
74 < 1    line and each probe checked only its own removal, so a line that fused two kept names
75 < 1    went green (n=1 run, 2026-09-29). `plan_check.py` prints a `SHARED fact:` line for each
76 < 1    such file.
77 < 1 4. **Prefer several small concurrent plans** landing on one main over one
78 < 1    large plan (0.26× batch wall, n=1 drain of 3 runs, #454, 2026-09-01). An effort split
79 < 1    across plans gives the **final** plan an integration-spanning acceptance — per-phase
80 < 1    green never establishes integrated green — or declares the gap explicitly in the final
81 < 1    plan. Never silently.
82 < 1 
83 < 1 ## Global Constraints discipline
84 < 1 
85 < 1 `## Global Constraints` holds the spec's binding, cross-cutting requirements: version
86 < 1 floors, naming and copy rules, platform requirements. State what must be true **of the
87 < 1 result**. Process rules — TDD ordering, commit cadence, "write the failing test first" —
88 < 1 are never Global Constraints: no diff evidences the order work was done in, so nothing
89 < 1 could ever check them.
90 < 1 
91 < 1 The section holds two kinds of bullet, and only one of them reaches the run. A `- Check:`
92 < 1 bullet is a command that runs across the whole run: the Flock reads the plan's `checks`
94 > 1 and the run settles green only when every one exits 0 (a `(minor)` suffix, which once meant
95 < 1 never run, is refused). A prose bullet is read by people only: nothing runs it and nothing forwards it,
93 < 2 and the run settles green only when every one exits 0 — unless it ends `(minor)`, which is
94 < 2 never run. A prose bullet is read by people only: nothing runs it and nothing forwards it,
95 < 1 and no builder sees it, because a builder's task body is its own `### Task` section and
96 < 1 nothing else. So a constraint a command can decide is written as a Check:, never as
97 < 1 prose — prose is where the undecidable half goes, and a constraint a builder must honour is
98 < 1 repeated in the Context of every task it touches. A prose
99 < 1 bullet naming a byte-identical file or a script's output is one the driver could have run,
100 < 1 so write it as a `Check:` beside the prose. Such a comparison has a base to compare
101 < 1 against: a `Check:` or `Run:` that compares the tree against BASE writes `$ULTRA_BASE`,
102 < 1 which the driver sets, in the environment of every `Check:` and `Run:` it executes, to the
103 < 1 run's base sha — so `- Check: git diff --quiet $ULTRA_BASE -- fleet/` is writable without
104 < 1 knowing the sha, where a frozen `git hash-object` literal is the shape for a single file.
105 < 1 And a `Check:` that runs a sim is paid by every task on every pass, where the same command
106 < 1 in the owning task's `Run:` is paid once: put it there, and keep this section for what no
107 < 1 single task owns. That is not only advice: a `Check:` whose command names a file one task's
108 < 1 Files own is refused by `plan_check.py`, naming the task and the path, because a check a single
109 < 1 task would turn green was never run-wide. A `Check:` that freezes a pathspec covering any
110 < 1 task's `Create:`, `Modify:` or `Delete:` path is refused by `plan_check.py` the
111 < 1 same way, because it goes red the moment that task's own patch lands (run-199, n=1 run,
112 < 1 2026-09-21) — freeze files, not the directory they sit in.
113 < 1 
114 < 1 The Flock, the one engine (the default since 0.3.39, the only one since map #1292 rule 8), selects no existing tests: a Flock run's proof is the
115 < 1 plan's probes and `Check:` lines and nothing else (operator, 2026-09-27). So a plan whose change
116 < 1 can break behaviour the repository already tests names those tests itself, as one `Check:` that
117 < 1 runs them (`- Check: python3 -m pytest -q tests/test_fleet_suite.py -k launch` for a launcher
118 < 1 change) — no single task owns them, so the check is run-wide, and a run that breaks them does not
119 < 1 settle green.
